On Aug 19, 10:22 pm, Henrik Bengtsson <h...@stat.berkeley.edu> wrote:
> Hi,
>
> my best guess is that you are picking up old result files, because you
> first processed the data using one CDF and then switched to another.
> The error generated is protecting you against further mistakes.  When
> you change annotation data you should add a tag so that the fullname,
> and hence the directory names, of your processed data reflects this.
>
> Example:
>
> cdf <- AffymetrixCdfFile$byChipType("HG-U133A", tag="Hs_REFSEQ")
> cs <- AffymetrixCelSet$byName("test", cdf=cdf)
>
> [Please use by<Nnn>() since from<Nnn>() are deprecated.]
>
> bc <- RmaBackgroundCorrection(cs, tags="*,refseq");
> csBC <- process(bc, verbose=verbose);
> qn <- QuantileNormalization(csBC, typesToUpdate="pm");
> csN <- process(qn, verbose=verbose);
> plm <- RmaPlm(csN);
> fit(plm, verbose=verbose);
> ces <- getChipEffectSet(plm)
>
> This will cause the 'csBC' data set and all following outputs to have
> a "refseq" tag.
>
> FYI, it is probably ok (approximately the same) to reuse the RMA bg
> correction and the quantile normalization results from previous run
> and introduce the tag first in the probe-level summarization, e.g.
>
> bc <- RmaBackgroundCorrection(cs);
> csBC <- process(bc, verbose=verbose);
> qn <- QuantileNormalization(csBC, typesToUpdate="pm");
> csN <- process(qn, verbose=verbose);
> plm <- RmaPlm(csN, tags="*,refseq");
> fit(plm, verbose=verbose);
> ces <- getChipEffectSet(plm)
>
> That should be faster to process.
>
> Cheers,
>
> /Henrik
>

